\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Search flow under one charged outer time budget. The solid path shows successful candidate generation. Search also stops if no optional location can be removed. An infeasible or interrupted repair skips ordering and preserves the stored feasible best. Only improvements observed by the deadline qualify, even if cleanup returns later. The dashed side box identifies the insertion-MIP component comparison; it is not an additional stage in the tested greedy-repair search.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hybrid-flow}{{1}{6}{}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.1}Destruction and feasibility repair}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Illustrative destruction and repair, not a benchmark outcome. Squares are mandatory locations, circles are optional locations, and the diamond is a demand. Filled visit nodes belong to the cycle; solid lines are routing edges and dashed links show active coverage. Let $p_a=p_b=p_c=1$, $p_u=2$, $p_v=3$, $P=5$, and $N_w=\{u,v\}$. Removing $u$ preserves three mandatory visits but violates both requirements. One feasible repair inserts $v$, restoring coverage and the prize quota. The drawing does not assert that every randomized repair selects $v$ or that an improvement is guaranteed.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:repair-example}{{2}{7}{}{figure.2}{}}
